<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gauda (region)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gauda was a region of ancient and medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, roughly the north-western part of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a> and neighbouring Bangladesh. In the sixth century its people, the Gaudas, were rivals of the Maukharis, and under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shashanka">Shashanka</a> in the early seventh century Gauda became a powerful kingdom. Later the name was used for Bengal as a whole and for the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a>, a capital of Bengali rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gauda-region.jpg" alt="Gauda (region)" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W. H. Allen and Co. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope1880BengalPres2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
